// Who this build is signed as, and what it calls itself.
//
// Both are per-machine facts, not project ones: they name a particular Apple developer
// account and a bundle id registered against it. They used to sit in `project.yml`, so every
// commit carried them and pulling someone else's change quietly reassigned your signing team.
//
// The values below are deliberately placeholders. Real ones go in `Signing.local.xcconfig`
// next to this file, which is gitignored — see the `#include?` at the bottom. Nothing here
// is secret (a Team ID is visible in any distributed app), it just has no business being
// shared or fought over in a diff.
//
// Kept as a config file rather than in the Xcode UI for the reason the old `project.yml`
// comment gave: settings made through Xcode live only in `project.pbxproj`, which XcodeGen
// rewrites from scratch on every `xcodegen generate`, so the signing team silently vanished
// each time the project was regenerated and the next device build failed with "requires a
// development team". XcodeGen never rewrites this file — it only references it.

// Empty is the correct default, not a made-up team id. Simulator builds need no team at all,
// so a fresh clone builds and tests without one; a device build fails with Xcode's own
// "requires a development team", which is a far better prompt than mysteriously signing
// against an account you don't have.
DEVELOPMENT_TEAM =

// `PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER` is composed from this in `project.yml` rather than set here,
// because the app and the test bundle need different ones and this file applies to both.
VVDEMUS_BUNDLE_ID_PREFIX = com.example.vvdemus

// Local overrides. `#include?` is the optional form — no error when the file is absent, which
// is exactly the fresh-clone case. Last assignment wins in an xcconfig, so this has to come
// after the defaults above or it would be the one being overridden.
#include? "Signing.local.xcconfig"
